A||||15||Scott2M4||Motor oil questions - trying to summarize||||5||
Z||000000||Scott2M4||01-14-2000||10:23 AM||||After doing a search on <B>OIL</B>, and sifting through a ton of sp<B>oil</B>er and c<B>oil</B> threads, I ended up with about three threads that cover the topic of oil changes.  Since there seemed to be discrepancies between those threads, I thought I'd try to get the topic summarized here (in case future users - like myself - find themselves utterly confused about what to put in their engine).<P>Synthetic or Conventional - I know the differences between both and that each has pros and cons.  The questions I have (which seem to be asked often) are (1) should it or shouldn't it be used in an older engine and (2) should it or shouldn't it be used in an engine that's been using conventional for quite awhile?<P>Oil weights - 16 years ago, Pontiac recommended using 5W30 oil in the Fiero.  What about now?  Many of our engines have over 100,000 miles on them.  They're obviously worn and have much more clearance than when they were new.  Should a different weight of oil be used now?  What about seasonal considerations?  Should 5W30 be used during the cold months and a different weight oil, say, 10W30 during the hot months?  Can "heavier" oils really cure (temporarily???) noisy engines or engines plagued by low compression?<P>Oil additives - Should they or shouldn't they be used?  Do they do more harm than good?<P>Obviously, I'm going to end up with lots and lots of opinions.  I guess my goal here is to get a "majority" vote.||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||01-14-2000||11:12 AM||||Stick with the 5W30. Sure, you can dump 50W oil in an old clunker and it solves some problems for a while, but just compounds other problems.<P>Synthetic vs. non-synthetic...Can't imagine why anyone still uses non-synthetic...best for hot running engines like ours. And if it leaks onto a hot engine part, it won't catch fire or smoke...actually easier to find leaks with synthetic (IMO) cause it stays fluid and doesn't vaporize.<P>For the extra protection and fuel economy it provides, synthetic (or even part synthetic like Syntec Blend) is worth the extra money...it saves money in the long run.<P>As to whether it leaks more than conventional, I just sold my '88 Dodge Shadow that had 219,000 miles on it...I've used Mobil 1 0W30 in it year round for last two years (5W30 prior to that).<P>That car got much better mileage with the 0W30 with no increase in oil consumption (may have actually decreased).<P>Whatever you use, change on a regular basis and always change the filter when you change the oil. ||||reg||10
Z||000002||deceler8||01-14-2000||11:14 AM||||Oh, forgot...most oil additives are trash.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-14-2000||11:53 AM||||I use regular non synthetic 5w30, all year round. Synthetic is more fluid at cold temperatures(o degrees f.), but I also don't want to find any more oil leaks on my car, other than the one I know of. Synthetic may showm more, it may not. Synthetic doesn't break down due to heat, so it has a much longer usefull life, only needs changing due to contamination, not breakdown. With 152,000 miles on my Fiero, I'm just going to stick with conventional oil, and changes every 3000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||01-14-2000||12:23 PM||||One thing to remember is that even conventional oil is much better now than it was when our cars were built...the new SJ oil is much better than the SF (or was it SG) oil recommended in our owners manuals...better resistance to thermal breakdown and much better at minimizing internal friction.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||01-14-2000||01:20 PM||||deceler8, you got more than 200,000 miles from a Chrysler 2.2/2.5?  That's amazing - and the best argument I've yet heard for synthetic oil!<P>For what it's worth, my opinion is stick with 5W30, oil additives are worthless, and synthetic oil is better than conventional.  A note of caution: synthetic oil can cause (or rather, reveal) minor oil leaks in older engines.  Synth oil is highly detergent, and if you've got some sludge caked around gaskets (such as oil pan and valve covers), the synth oil may break it down and let oil begin to leak there.<P>I had heard this from several people, but never experienced it myself until I tried synthetic oil in my old Honda CB350 motorcycle.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave85||01-14-2000||09:14 PM||||Synthetic is the way to go.  My car ran a whole lot smoother and much cooler with the synthetic. However, I had to switch back because of the oil leaks. I'm not quite ready to do the rebuild yet and don't want to tear it apart twice.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||01-15-2000||01:00 AM||||<BR>My ‘87 2.5L has over 200,000 kilometres and it runs great. Although I never see any blue smoke, it does go through some oil between changes. Because of the engine wear, I use 10W-40 in the cooler months and 20W-50 in the heat of the summer to cut down on oil consumption. I believe the only drawback to using heavier oil in an older engine is the resistance that the heavier oil itself produces. This might reduce the miles per gallon a wee bit. If I had a newly rebuilt engine, I would use synthetic oil, but to use it in a worn engine would be a mistake. Synthetic oil is so thin that if an engine has a tendency to burn oil, it would be worse. This may seem to fly in the face of what deceler8 has stated, but it's possible that his high mileage engine was less worn than mine was when I bought the car. The lifespan and durability of an engine is probably determined in the first few miles of it's life by how it's treated during the "break in" period.<P>Regarding oil additives, we had a thread about that quite awhile back. Check it out at:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000105.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000105.html</A> <P>I wrote about my experiences with Dura-Lube.||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||01-15-2000||10:42 AM||||I used Mobil 1 in my '88 Shadow 2.2 from its first oil change at 2500 miles to the day I sold it. The guy I sold it to has put another 10,000 miles on it and says it still runs great. No engine repairs whatsoever on that 2.2.<P>Also used Mobil 1 in the 5 speed trans and Mobil 1 synthetic grease. Never had to replace any suspension parts that were lubed with the stuff. <P>Also used Mobil 1 in my 1951 Chevy 3100 truck, but that stuff comes out the rear main seal, so I switched to part synthetic. The old rope-type seal on really old cars (especially those that have two-part seals) really don't work well with synthetic. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||01-15-2000||06:36 PM||||When I got my Civic, it had about 30,000 miles on it and it would use a quart of oil between changes-usually about 5,000 miles. I switched to Mobil 1 and after 100,000 more miles, it still only uses a quart between changes-every 7,000 miles. With over 130,000 miles on the engine, inside the valve cover is still clean, shiny aluminum with no buildup at all. I use it in all my cars- I just hate changing oil in my 'Burb with the 8 quart pan, it gets expensive!||||reg||1
Z||000010||la9||01-15-2000||07:59 PM||||Here is a link I found about oil additives like slick 50. It wasn't the article I was looking for but it looks close.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.msgroup.org/TIP043.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.msgroup.org/TIP043.html</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||01-16-2000||01:09 AM||||Ok, I'll give my unsolicited testimonial.<P>I've run Mobil 1 through 5 engines. Three were 660 engines and all exceeded 250k miles. I did change the timing chain in two only because it wears regardless of the oil. The third I let run until 280k and the chain broken. 660's don't like that. Tends to reshape valve stems.<P>Was it the Mobil 1 that made them run that long? Hard to say, but when I tore them down, they were spot-on clean inside and the bearings looks great. One did have a small piece of debris in it (looked like block flashing), but all otherwise looked great.<P>A friend has one of those cool oil additive testers "as seen on TV". The motor and the clamp for using bearing pellets and a torque wrench. We tested tons of different stuff and together or seperate, Mobil 1 and Prolong would not allow the bearing to be stalled. We could lift the dang box off the bench with the wrench and it would keep running. We could leave it for hours at full torque and it kept running. We did not wear smocks to keep the test fair.<P>So I run Mobil 1 and Prolong. No way to know if it helped. I do replace it every 3-5k miles although I've run it as long as 8k.<P>Finally, I've noticed Mobil's tendency to remove sludge from non-synthetic engines. This can be either good or bad. We've never seen a problem, but Mobil will clean the engine out after about 5 changes. Bad seals could show up them. I've never seen an engine with "good" seals start leaking.<P>Sometimes I think the engines run smoother on synthetic and Prolong, but it's a real tough call. Could be the newly washed car affect.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||olympic||01-16-2000||01:36 AM||||     I run castrol syntec 10W30 in my car and a semi-synthetic in my business vehicles. I've heard several people claim that they get better mileage(2-3 mpg) from a synthetic oil.  Heres some insight, I worked for another taxi company a few years back. He used the cheapest oil he could find and I regularely saw cars with 12000 miles(plus a few hundred hours of idling time) on the same oil and filter. He's been in business quite a few years and has never had to replace an engine. The worst thing I saw happen was the valve seals would wear out and the car would start to smoke.  He always put 300,000 miles on the car before selling it. One car had 500,000 miles. I don't know if this shows how good todays oil is or how well engines are built now.||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||01-16-2000||01:56 AM||||Olympic,<P>Yeah, that's the problem. It's hard to tell. I think the engines are far better in the 80's and later than prior to that. <P>The only thing I can swear to is the lack of build up in the engine for synthetics.<P>Also, Castrol Syntec is a good oil too. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||01-16-2000||12:48 PM||||I dont know how good bulk fleet grade diesel oil is but an old friend who ran a truck tire repair service had pretty good luck with it. His over the road repairs vehicle was a 70's chevrolet van. It had over 400,000 miles on it before the cam wore out. All they ever used in it was the 15w40 truck oil. He replace the cam and one lifter and put it back on the road. It is still running 5 years later, but the odometer broke so we dont know how many miles are on it now.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierosound||01-17-2000||02:39 PM||||For everything you always wanted to know about oil...  but were afraid to ask...<P>Check out this website...<P> <A HREF="http://www.rconcepts.com/beard/dragnet/drag/oilinfo.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rconcepts.com/beard/dragnet/drag/oilinfo.html</A> <P>||||reg||10
